// Plain-English (?) tooltips for Engineer mode. One entry per line: X(Id, "text").
// Rule: 1-2 short sentences, first says what it measures, second what a good or bad value is.
// ASCII only (no hex escapes) so the strings stay readable in any editor.

// ---- Cockpit ----
X(OledDisplay, "The robot's status glass: what it is doing, which maze cell and compass heading it is in, the run time and speed. Good: it follows the maze; 'Stuck' or 'Crashed' means the firmware needs attention.")
X(Leds, "The four status lights the firmware drives (green, cyan, amber, red). The firmware decides what they mean; a red light usually means an error.")
X(Buttons, "The two push buttons on the robot: hold one to press it. 'Hold at boot' presses it while the firmware starts, like holding a button while powering on.")
X(MotorPwm, "How much voltage the firmware asks of each motor, as a share of the battery (PWM duty), left (L_PWM) and right (R_PWM). Good: below the cap; an amber bar means the 6 V limit is clipping the request.")
X(MotorCap, "The most voltage a motor may get: 6.0 V for N20 motors on a 2-cell pack, shown as a share of the battery. Never raise it above 6 V on real hardware.")
X(SensorBars, "One bar per distance sensor: the raw reading, the same value scaled 0 to 1, and the distance in mm. A long bar means a wall is close; 'no wall' means nothing is in range.")
X(ImuEncoders, "Turn rate from the gyro (degrees per second), acceleration, and the wheel encoder counts. Good: gyro near 0 when standing still, and both encoder counts rising together when driving straight.")
X(Events, "The run's milestones and problems with the simulated time they happened. Click a row to jump the timeline there; red rows are errors or wall hits.")
X(Attitude, "Where the robot points, like a compass: north is up, east is right. The needle is the robot's heading in degrees clockwise from north; the cyan tick is the direction the firmware is steering toward.")
X(GyroRate, "How fast the robot is turning, from the gyro, in degrees per second (positive = counter-clockwise). Good: near 0 when driving straight; large values only while turning.")
X(DetailsLog, "A timestamped diary of the run: the moves the robot makes, the firmware's own log lines and any errors. Use the pills to focus, search to find a phrase, Copy log to paste it into a bug report.")

// ---- Maze Studio ----
X(HeatPeakSpeed, "The fastest speed the robot reached in each cell. Good: bright cells where the maze allows speed; a dark long straight means time is being left on the table.")
X(HeatGrip, "How hard the tyres were worked in each cell, as a share of the grip limit (mu x g). Good: below about 85 percent; near 100 percent means the robot is about to slide.")
X(HeatPotential, "Turn-weighted potential: the cost of reaching the goal from each cell, where every turn counts as extra distance. Good: a smooth fall toward the goal; hot cells are close, cold cells are far.")
X(RoutePreview, "The shortest route from start to goal on the true maze, not what the firmware knows yet. Blue legs are straight, amber legs are diagonal chains the robot could cut across.")
X(MazeLayers, "Switch what is drawn on the maze: the grid, distances, coordinates, the walls and colours the firmware reports, the path trace and the crash markers.")
X(DeckRunAccel, "The cruise speed (mm/s) and acceleration (mm/s2) assumed for the planned speed run. Faster is quicker but harder on the tyres; the Peak lateral figure tells you when it is too much.")
X(DeckMu, "Tyre mu: the grip between tyre and floor. Clean rubber is roughly 0.6 to 1.0; use a lower number if your robot slides in corners.")
X(DeckDiagonals, "Let the plan cut straight across staircase corners instead of turning at every cell. Quicker, but it needs more grip and tighter clearances.")
X(DeckSummary, "Predicted run time, distance, number of moves (and how many are diagonal), and the highest sideways acceleration. Good: Peak lateral below the tyre mu; above it the robot would slide.")
X(DeckTable, "The speed run broken into moves: each row is a straight, curve or diagonal with its length, speed, time and cornering g. A red g means that corner is beyond the tyre grip.")
X(InspectorBfs, "How many cells you must cross to reach the goal if turns cost nothing. Lower is closer.")
X(InspectorCost, "Turn-weighted cost to the goal: distance plus a penalty for every turn. Lower is better; 'no route' means that cell is walled off.")
X(InspectorPeakSpeed, "The fastest the robot went while in this cell. Good: close to the planned run speed on straights.")
X(InspectorLateral, "The strongest sideways acceleration in this cell, in g. Good: well below the tyre mu; near mu means it nearly slid.")
X(InspectorGrip, "How much of the tyre grip was used in this cell (sideways and forward together). Good: under 85 percent; 100 percent is the sliding limit.")
X(InspectorClearance, "The closest the robot's centre came to a wall in this cell. Good: 20 mm or more; under 10 mm means it is skimming the wall.")

// ---- Telemetry and Compare ----
X(TelemetryGroups, "Pick which plots to show: Motion (speed and turn rate), Control (steering and error signals), Power (motor volts, amps, battery) and Sensors (distance readings).")
X(TelemetryWindow, "How many seconds of history the plots show. Shorter shows more detail, longer shows the whole run.")
X(TelemetryFollow, "Keep the plots scrolling with the live run. Turn it off to zoom and pan the recorded data.")
X(TelemetryExport, "Export view CSV saves only what is on screen; Export all CSV saves every recorded channel, ready for MATLAB or a spreadsheet.")
X(TelemetryTimeline, "Drag to look back through the recorded run. 'Rewind sim to cursor' makes the robot really go back to that moment (same seed, same result); Live returns to now.")
X(PlotLinear, "The speed the firmware asked for (v_set) against the speed measured (v) in mm/s. Good: the measured line hugs the request; lag or ripple means the speed loop needs tuning.")
X(PlotAngular, "The turn rate asked for (w_set) against the measured turn rate in degrees per second. Good: they overlap; a big gap means the robot turns slower than asked.")
X(PlotSteering, "The steering correction and the speed and rotation errors from the control loop. Good: small and centred on 0; big swings mean the robot is fighting to stay straight.")
X(PlotMotorVoltage, "Voltage on the left and right motor. Good: below the 6 V cap; flat tops mean the motor is at its limit and cannot give more.")
X(PlotMotorCurrent, "Current each motor draws, in amps. Good: short peaks while accelerating; long high values heat the driver and sag the battery.")
X(PlotBattery, "Battery voltage over the run. Good: stays above 6.0 V; a dip toward 6 V means the run is draining the pack too hard.")
X(PlotSensors, "The distance each sensor reports in mm. Good: steady in a corridor; spikes or sudden 'no wall' gaps mean a side opening or a noisy sensor.")
X(PlotCustom, "Plots you picked under Channels..., for example your own hal_telemetry values.")
X(CompareRuns, "A is the live run, B is a saved run from the runs folder. Pick a B, then see where one run wins time over the other at the same distance.")
X(CompareDt, "dt(s) is the time difference between run B and run A at the same point on the track (t_B minus t_A). Above 0 the live run A is ahead; below 0 it is behind, and the dips show where.")
X(CompareSpeed, "Speed of both runs against distance travelled, so you can see which corners one run takes faster than the other.")
X(CompareChips, "'Common' is the length of track both runs covered; the next chip is the final time gap over that length, and the last is a one-line verdict.")
X(GhostReplay, "The saved run B drawn on the maze as an amber ghost beside the live robot, so you can see where the two paths differ.")
X(Traction, "Traction circle: each dot is one moment of the run, sideways grip (left-right) against speeding-up or braking grip (up-down), in g. Dots inside the red circle (mu x g) are within the tyre limit; outside means sliding.")
X(TractionPeak, "The highest grip demand in the run as a share of the limit (mu x g). Good: below 85 percent; over 100 percent means the tyres would have slipped.")

// ---- Tuning and Reports ----
X(MountFront, "How far the front sensors point outward (toe-out) from straight ahead. A little helps see side walls early; too much and they miss the wall in front.")
X(MountSide, "The angle of the side sensors from straight ahead. Around 45 degrees spots side openings early; nearer 90 degrees reads the closest wall more directly.")
X(MountRays, "How many sample rays each sensor beam uses. More rays give a smoother reading but a slower simulation; 5 is a good balance.")
X(MountBeam, "The width of each sensor beam in degrees. Narrow beams give clean distances; wide beams blur corners and openings.")
X(MountApply, "Rebuilds the robot with the new sensor angles and restarts the run, so every result stays reproducible (the seed is unchanged).")
X(BatteryPack, "Battery voltage and charge left. Good: green and above 6.0 V; amber or red means the motors lose power or the robot may reset.")
X(BatteryMotorAmps, "Current drawn by one motor, in amps, against about 2.6 A stall. Good: short peaks; a long full bar means the motor is working at its limit.")
X(BrownoutMargin, "How many volts the battery sits above the 6.0 V reset line. Good: more than 0.8 V; under 0.3 V the robot may reset during a hard acceleration.")
X(PackCurrent, "The total current the battery supplies (motors plus electronics). Good: bursts of a few amps; long high current sags the voltage.")
X(ChargeUsed, "How much of the battery's capacity this run has used, in milliamp-hours. Good: a small fraction of the pack; running out ends the run.")
X(Junctions, "Estimated chip temperatures of the 5 V converter (U17), the 3.3 V regulator (U18) and the motor driver (U13). Good: below 85 C; above 105 C risks thermal shutdown.")
X(MissionMin, "The predicted lowest battery voltage over a full 3-run mission, and its margin above 6 V. Good: a positive margin; a negative one means the pack would brown out.")
X(StepSource, "Which signal to analyse for a step response, and how many seconds after the first step to look at.")
X(StepRise, "Rise 10-90 percent: the time the signal takes to climb from 10 to 90 percent of its final value. Good: short (the speed loop should be well under 0.2 s); long means a sluggish response.")
X(StepSettling, "Settling 2 percent: the time until the signal stays within 2 percent of its final value. Good: close to the rise time; much longer means ringing or slow creep.")
X(StepOvershoot, "How far the signal went past its final value, in percent. Good: under 5 percent; over 20 percent means the controller is too aggressive.")
X(ImuSamples, "How many gyro readings were taken while the robot stood still. More samples give a steadier estimate.")
X(ImuBias, "The average gyro reading while standing still: the offset added to every turn rate. Good: near 0; a large bias makes the heading drift.")
X(ImuNoise, "IMU 1-sigma: the typical size of the random jitter on the gyro reading (one standard deviation). Good: under 0.3 deg/s; more noise makes the heading wobble.")
X(ImuPeak, "The largest swing between the lowest and highest gyro reading while still. Good: about 6 times the 1-sigma noise; far more means spikes.")
X(ImuConfigured, "The bias this robot config was told to have; compare it with the measured bias to check the simulation.")
X(ImuHistogram, "How often each gyro value occurred while standing still. Good: a narrow bell centred near 0; a lopsided or wide bell means bias or high noise.")
X(ReportsExport, "Save a printable HTML report, a MATLAB-ready CSV bundle, every recorded channel as CSV, or a screenshot. Files go to your MMSim data folder.")
X(ReportChecks, "Pass or fail checks on the run: goal reached, no wall crashes, motor voltage cap, brownout margin and traction. Good: all PASS; 'not yet' only means the run has not got that far.")
X(RunRecord, "The exact ingredients of this run: maze, config, firmware, seed and a fingerprint of the path. The same record always gives the same trajectory fingerprint.")

// ---- Robot config editor ----
X(CfgFile, "Which robot config file is loaded. Edits apply to a copy in memory; the original file is never changed, use Save copy to keep your edits.")
X(CfgMass, "The robot's mass in grams. Lighter robots accelerate faster but grip less; a typical classic mouse is 80 to 150 g.")
X(CfgWheel, "Wheel radius in mm. Bigger wheels give a higher top speed but weaker acceleration and coarser encoder steps.")
X(CfgTrack, "Distance between the two wheel contact points in mm. A wider track turns more stably but needs more room in corners.")
X(CfgVoltage, "The most voltage a motor may get. N20 motors on a 2-cell pack must stay at or below 6.0 V; the simulator allows more, but a real robot would overheat.")
X(CfgSlip, "Turns the tyre slip model on. Off means perfect grip; on lets the tyres slide when the demand passes mu x g.")
X(CfgMu, "Tyre mu: grip between tyre and floor. Clean rubber is roughly 0.6 to 1.0; it only matters when the tyre slip model is on.")
X(CfgBatteryModel, "On: battery voltage sags under load and drives the motors (realistic). Off: an ideal supply that never droops.")
X(CfgRint, "The battery's internal resistance in ohms. Higher values make the voltage sag more under load; a fresh small LiPo is around 0.1 to 0.2 ohm.")
X(CfgSensorRange, "How far every distance sensor can see, in mm. Longer range sees walls earlier but reads less precisely at the far end.")
X(CfgSensorNoise, "Random error added to every sensor reading: a fixed part (in sensor units) and a part that grows with distance (percent). Good: small; high noise makes wall detection flicker.")
X(CfgApply, "Apply & Restart rebuilds the robot with your numbers and restarts the run (same seed), so results stay reproducible.")
X(CfgSaveCopy, "Saves your edited robot as a new JSON file; an existing file is never overwritten, so generic_classic.json stays untouched.")

// ---- Race timer ----
X(TimerPreset, "The official run budget: 3, 5 or 7 minutes. Pick one before starting; the clock counts down from it.")
X(TimerBasis, "Real time counts like a stopwatch beside the course, whatever the simulation speed. Sim time counts only simulated seconds, so Fast mode runs the clock faster.")
X(TimerControls, "Start begins the countdown (and resumes after Pause), Reset puts it back to the full time. With 'Start with GO' the timer starts when you press GO, and time-up stops the run.")
X(TimerAudio, "A quiet tick each second for the last ten seconds and a longer tone at zero. Follows the sound mute switch (M).")
X(AttemptTable, "One row per run: time to reach the goal (search), the speed-run time, wall crashes, and your best speed run so far. A run counts when the speed run finishes, or when you restart or time runs out.")
